lessons.content_markdown:
## Gestures on iOS

The same W3C Actions API works across platforms, but iOS has its own quirks:

```js
// Swipe using mobile: extension (recommended over raw Actions on iOS)
await driver.execute('mobile: swipe', { direction: 'up' });

// Handle system permission alerts (Face ID, camera, notifications)
await driver.execute('mobile: alert', { action: 'accept' });
```

### Simulator-only limitations
- Push notifications require `xcrun simctl push` to simulate, not real APNs delivery
- Face ID/Touch ID: use `xcrun simctl` to 'enroll' and 'match'/'no match' a simulated biometric in the Simulator; behaves differently than a real fingerprint sensor
- Location simulation: `driver.execute('mobile: setSimulatedLocation', {...})` works great on Simulator, more restricted on real devices

Always validate biometric- and push-heavy flows on **real devices** before shipping — Simulator behavior is a reasonable proxy, not a guarantee.

## The Screen Object + Platform Map Pattern

Instead of two entirely separate test suites, wrap platform differences behind a single interface:

```js
class LoginScreen {
  constructor(driver) {
    this.driver = driver;
    this.isAndroid = driver.capabilities.platformName === 'Android';
  }

  get emailField() {
    return this.isAndroid
      ? this.driver.$('id=com.zaka.app:id/emailInput')
      : this.driver.$('~emailInput');
  }

  get loginButton() {
    return this.isAndroid
      ? this.driver.$('id=com.zaka.app:id/loginButton')
      : this.driver.$('~loginButton');
  }

  async login(email, password) {
    await (await this.emailField).setValue(email);
    await (await this.driver.$('~passwordInput')).setValue(password);
    await (await this.loginButton).click();
  }
}
```

The test itself never mentions 'Android' or 'iOS' — it just calls `loginScreen.login(...)`, and the same test file runs against both platforms in CI by swapping capabilities.

