}

lessons.content_markdown:
## Native vs. WebView Context

Many apps embed web content (a checkout flow, help center, etc.) inside a `WebView`. Appium requires you to explicitly switch context to interact with it.

```js
const contexts = await driver.getContexts();
// e.g. ['NATIVE_APP', 'WEBVIEW_com.zaka.app']

await driver.switchContext('WEBVIEW_com.zaka.app');
// Now standard web locators (CSS) work:
const el = await driver.$('#checkout-button');
await el.click();

await driver.switchContext('NATIVE_APP'); // switch back for native elements
```

### Common gotchas
- The WebView context only appears **after** the WebView has actually loaded content — poll `getContexts()` if you switch too early
- Android WebViews require the app to have `WebView.setWebContentsDebuggingEnabled(true)` in debug builds, or Appium can't attach to them via Chromedriver

lessons.starter_code:
const contexts = await driver.getContexts();
console.log(contexts); // ['NATIVE_APP', 'WEBVIEW_com.zaka.app']

await driver.switchContext('WEBVIEW_com.zaka.app');
const checkoutBtn = await driver.$('#checkout-button');
await checkoutBtn.click();

await driver.switchContext('NATIVE_APP');

// Android: grant all needed permissions up front
const capabilities = {
  platformName: 'Android',
  'appium:automationName': 'UiAutomator2',
  'appium:app': './app/app-debug.apk',
  'appium:autoGrantPermissions': true,
};

// iOS: handle an unexpected system alert mid-test
try {
  await driver.execute('mobile: alert', { action: 'accept' });
} catch (e) {
  // no alert present, continue
}

lessons.content_markdown:
## Why Cloud Device Farms

Running against one local emulator doesn't scale, and real device coverage (dozens of OS/screen combos) is impractical to own physically. Services like **BrowserStack App Automate**, **Sauce Labs**, and **LambdaTest** host real devices and expose the same WebDriver-compatible Appium endpoint.

```js
// wdio.conf.js — pointing at BrowserStack instead of localhost
exports.config = {
  user: process.env.BROWSERSTACK_USERNAME,
  key: process.env.BROWSERSTACK_ACCESS_KEY,
  hostname: 'hub.browserstack.com',
  capabilities: [{
    platformName: 'Android',
    'appium:app': 'bs://<uploaded-app-id>',
    'bstack:options': {
      deviceName: 'Samsung Galaxy S23',
      osVersion: '13.0',
    },
  }],
};
```

Your test code doesn't change at all — only the connection config. This is one of Appium's biggest strengths: the same suite runs locally, in CI, and against real cloud devices unmodified.

